Subject: Packed arrays: reads, stores and appends of numbers stay in the packed kind, and `[]` starts packed (QJS_PACKED_ARRAYS)

What this does
  - Only with `QJS_PACKED_ARRAYS` on. `[]` (the array literal with no elements) and `new Array()` start as
    kind int32 without storage; a number appended keeps the array packed, any other value unpacks it
    (free when it has no storage yet). Every other creator of arrays still makes generic arrays.
  - Reads: `js_get_fast_array_element`, the register VM's `get_element` handler and `qjs_aot_get_element`
    read a packed element as an int32 or a float64 (`__JS_NewFloat64`; -0 stays -0).
  - Stores below the length: an int32 (or an integral float64 in int32 range, not -0) into kind int32 and
    any number into kind float64 are stores in place; any other number into kind int32 WIDENS the array
    to float64 (the storage moves); a non-number unpacks first. `regvm_put_elem_fast` takes only the
    cases that need no allocation and leaves the rest to `JS_SetPropertyValue`, which has the error path.
  - Appends (`push`, `a[a.length] = x`, `add_fast_array_element`): a number keeps the kind (growth
    reallocates in the kind's element size, at least 4 elements, 1.5x growth as for generic arrays); the
    first non-number unpacks and the rest is appended generically. `push` keeps `length` consistent when
    an allocation fails half way.
  - Shrinking `length` of a packed array only lowers the count (there is nothing to release).

Why we need it
  The interpreter and the AOT runtime then read and write number arrays without a JSValue per element, and
  the generator can emit typed loads and stores on them. Everything else sees a packed array only
  through the kind-aware paths (a kind-aware read, or unpack at a mutator with an error path).

Pointer hazard
  Widening moves `values` exactly as unpacking does. The sites that can widen are the packed store
  (`js_arr_packed_store`, from `JS_SetPropertyValue`) and the packed append (`js_arr_packed_append`, from
  `add_fast_array_element`, the `push` fast path); none of them is reached while a caller holds a pointer
  into the elements: callers that hold a `JSValue *` get it from `js_get_fast_array`/`qjs_aot_dense`, which
  refuse a packed array, and everything else re-reads the storage per element. packed1.js widens the
  array it iterates from forEach/map/sort/filter/reduce/indexOf/concat callbacks under asan, flag on, with
  QJS_PACKED_TEST=0 and 1.
  Creators: `JS_NewArray` is unchanged (generic), so the builtin results and the arguments of rest
  parameters, Object.keys/entries and the other engine-made arrays are generic; only `[]` and `Array()`
  produce kind 1. Any creator that wrote `values` of such an array directly would fail the assertion in
  JS_ARR_VALS (the renamed storage member routes every use through it); none does (pktest: 230 files, 0 differ,
  asan, both test modes).
